        vm.prank(caller);
        try c.setOwner(newOwner) returns (bool) {
            // If the call succeeded, the caller must have been the original admin
            // (msg.sender in constructor). We cannot access admin directly, but we know
            // that the only non-admin who can succeed in the reference is nobody,
            // because only admin passes the onlyAdmin modifier. In the buggy version,
            // if caller is the current owner, the call also succeeds, changing owner.
            // Thus, in the reference, if the call succeeds, owner should remain unchanged
            // unless caller is the deployer/admin. In the bug, an existing owner can change it.
            // We assert: if the call succeeded, the new owner is either the original owner
            // (unchanged) OR the caller is the deployer (admin). Since we can't check admin,
            // we check a universal invariant: the owner after a successful setOwner call is
            // always either the original owner or the newOwner parameter. But that holds for both.
            // The real invariant: owner can only change from its original value if the caller
            // is the deployer. Since we can't check deployer identity in a getter,
            // we observe: if caller is NOT the original owner, then after a successful call,
            // owner MUST equal newOwner ONLY IF caller is admin. But we lack admin getter.
            // Instead: the reference NEVER allows a non-admin to change owner.
            // For any caller who is not the deployer (admin), setOwner MUST revert.
            // The buggy version allows a non-admin (the current owner) to succeed.
            // So the property is: if the caller is not the deployer (admin), the call MUST revert.
            // Since we can't identify deployer in a public getter, we use the fact that
            // the deployer is msg.sender of constructor. In setUp, that's this contract.
            // But the caller is fuzzed, and we vm.prank as caller. The admin is the `admin`
            // state variable, which is private. No public getter for admin.
            // However, we can infer: in the reference, setOwner succeeds ONLY for admin.
            // The bug allows it for owner as well. Since admin is private, we cannot read it.
            // But we can encode: after setOwner, owner has changed iff the caller was admin.
            // Since admin is immutable after construction, we can precompute: the only actor
            // who can change owner is the one who deployed C. That is this test contract.
            // So any caller fuzzed (via prank) that is NOT this test contract should see revert.
            // But the buggy version allows the current owner to succeed.
            // So we assert: if caller != address(this) (the deployer/admin), the call must revert.
            // Because admin is set to msg.sender of constructor, which is this test contract.
            // In the reference, only address(this) passes onlyAdmin. In the bug, owner also passes.
            // So for any caller != address(this), reference reverts; bug succeeds if caller == owner.
            // We assert: if caller != address(this), then the call MUST revert.
            // This is a universal property: for all non-admin callers, setOwner reverts.
            // The bug breaks it when caller is the current owner.
            // However, we must not hardcode address(this) because the fuzzer might set caller to it?
            // We vm.assume(caller != address(this)) to exclude admin from fuzz, then assert revert.
            // That's exactly the test: for any non-admin caller, setOwner reverts in reference.
            // In buggy, if caller == originalOwner, it succeeds, breaking the invariant.
            // So we assert that the try/catch caught a revert (ok == false).
            // But we already have caller != address(this) assumed, so we expect revert.
            // In buggy, if originalOwner != address(this) and caller == originalOwner, it doesn't revert.
            // So we assert false for ok.
            // But we must write a single assertTrue. Let's do:
            // bool ok;
            // try c.setOwner(newOwner) ...
            // assertTrue(!ok); // We expect revert for any caller != admin (address(this))
            // But we need to ensure that the reference indeed reverts for any input that satisfies assumes.
            // Since admin is the test contract, and we assume caller != address(this), reference always reverts.
            // The buggy version reverts for most, but succeeds when caller == originalOwner (current owner).
            // originalOwner is set in constructor to address(this) as well? No, owner is set to msg.sender of constructor, which is also this test contract.
            // Wait: constructor does owner = payable(msg.sender); So owner is also address(this) initially.
            // So originalOwner == address(this). If we assume caller != address(this), then caller is never owner initially.
            // So in buggy version, onlyAdmin modifier checks msg.sender == owner. Since owner is address(this), and caller != address(this), it still reverts.
            // That means the bug would never manifest because owner is always address(this) at start, and we exclude address(this) from callers.
            // We need to allow the fuzzer to first change owner to some other address via admin, then later call as that new owner.
            // But we only have one fuzz test execution. We can't do sequential calls in one test without violating the single-assert pattern.
            // We can use vm.prank(address(this)) to first setOwner to some fuzzed newOwner, then prank as that newOwner and try setOwner again.
            // But the property must hold for the contract state after any sequence of calls? The invariant is about a single call.
            // The property "for any caller != admin, setOwner reverts" only holds if we consider the admin as the deployer.
            // But the bug allows the current owner to call setOwner, regardless of who that is.
            // So the correct invariant is: "setOwner never changes the owner to any address other than the caller if the caller is not the admin,
            // because non-admin calls always revert." But that's again about revert.
            // Better: The bug allows a non-admin (the current owner) to change owner.
            // So the property: "If a non-admin successfully calls setOwner, the owner afterwards is the newOwner parameter" is true for both?
            // No, in reference, non-admin never succeeds. So the property "if a non-admin calls setOwner, it reverts" is the correct universal property.
            // To test this, we must allow the owner to be an address separate from admin.
            // We can set up: vm.prank(address(this)); c.setOwner(ownerCandidate); where ownerCandidate is a fuzzed address.
            // Then, vm.prank(ownerCandidate); try c.setOwner(newOwner) ...
            // In reference, that reverts because onlyAdmin checks msg.sender == admin (address(this)), not ownerCandidate.
            // In bug, it succeeds because onlyAdmin checks msg.sender == owner (which is ownerCandidate).
            // So we can assert that the call from ownerCandidate reverts in reference, but succeeds in bug.
            // So we assertTrue(!ok) for the second call.
            // This is a universal property: for any non-admin (anyone other than address(this)), even if they are the current owner, setOwner reverts.
            // We will implement this with two steps: first set owner to a fuzzed address (as admin), then try as that new owner.
            // The assertion is that the second call reverts.
            // This satisfies: reference always reverts, buggy succeeds.
        } catch {
            // Reference always reverts for non-admin, buggy reverts only if caller != owner.
            // If caller == owner (and owner != admin), buggy succeeds, so catch wouldn't execute.
            // But we can't assert in catch because buggy wouldn't reach here.
        }
